## Proof

We claim the map $\varphi : k[x] \otimes_k k[x] \to k[x,y]$, defined on pure tensors by
$$\varphi(x^i \otimes x^j) = x^i y^j,$$
**is** a ring isomorphism. We verify the three required properties: it is a well-defined ring homomorphism, it is injective, and it is surjective.

### 1. Well-definedness and ring-homomorphism property

The tensor product $k[x] \otimes_k k[x]$ is a $k$-algebra with multiplication defined on simple tensors by
$$(f \otimes g)\cdot(h \otimes \ell) = fh \otimes g\ell,$$
and unit $1 \otimes 1$.

The set $\{x^i \otimes x^j : i,j \ge 0\}$ is a $k$-basis of $k[x]\otimes_k k[x]$ (since $\{x^i\}$ is a basis of $k[x]$, and tensor products of bases give bases). Hence specifying $\varphi$ on this basis determines a unique $k$-linear map, so $\varphi$ is well-defined as a $k$-linear map.

We check it respects multiplication. For basis elements:
$$\varphi\bigl((x^i\otimes x^j)(x^a\otimes x^b)\bigr) = \varphi(x^{i+a}\otimes x^{j+b}) = x^{i+a}y^{j+b},$$
while
$$\varphi(x^i\otimes x^j)\,\varphi(x^a\otimes x^b) = x^i y^j \cdot x^a y^b = x^{i+a}y^{j+b}.$$
These agree, so by $k$-bilinearity $\varphi$ respects multiplication on all elements. The unit is preserved:
$$\varphi(1\otimes 1) = x^0 y^0 = 1.$$
Thus $\varphi$ is a $k$-algebra homomorphism (hence a ring homomorphism).

### 2. Injectivity

The monomials $\{x^i y^j : i,j \ge 0\}$ form a $k$-basis of $k[x,y]$. The map $\varphi$ sends the basis $\{x^i\otimes x^j\}$ of $k[x]\otimes_k k[x]$ bijectively onto the basis $\{x^i y^j\}$ of $k[x,y]$. A $k$-linear map that carries a basis to a basis is a $k$-vector-space isomorphism; in particular it is injective.

### 3. Surjectivity

Every element of $k[x,y]$ is a finite $k$-linear combination of monomials $x^i y^j$, each of which is $\varphi(x^i \otimes x^j)$. Hence every element of $k[x,y]$ lies in the image of $\varphi$, so $\varphi$ is surjective.

### Conclusion

The map $\varphi$ is a ring homomorphism that is bijective as a $k$-linear map, hence a ring isomorphism. Therefore $k[x]\otimes_k k[x] \cong k[x,y]$ as $k$-algebras (and in particular as rings).

